WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
A transceiver sits at the very edge of the board,

2
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.060
where miswiring and static strike first

3
00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:10.440
SKU-5 puts two bus links on one die,

4
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.120
built for that edge.

5
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.975
The core logic is only a thin skin.

6
00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:19.195
Everything that decides how the part behaves on the wire is analog,

7
00:00:19.195 --> 00:00:21.795
on thick-oxide devices.

8
00:00:21.795 --> 00:00:25.502
How far a signal travels is set by how its edges rise

9
00:00:25.502 --> 00:00:26.542
and fall.

10
00:00:27.102 --> 00:00:31.102
So every edge is shaped, and every delay accounted for.

11
00:00:31.102 --> 00:00:36.739
The outputs drive a half-duplex bus with up to 256 nodes.

12
00:00:36.739 --> 00:00:41.919
Fast over a short run, or slow across 1.2 kilometres: two operating

13
00:00:41.919 --> 00:00:43.579
points, not one.

14
00:00:43.631 --> 00:00:46.851
The receiver reads the bus back with hysteresis,

15
00:00:46.851 --> 00:00:51.271
and a dead or open bus reads as a known state,

16
00:00:51.271 --> 00:00:54.571
never as noise the logic could take for data.

17
00:00:54.575 --> 00:00:58.175
The second channel repeats the same path for CAN-FD,

18
00:00:58.175 --> 00:01:02.395
with its own supply and clock recovery beside it.

19
00:01:02.596 --> 00:01:05.596
Each pin that leaves the board brings its own protection.

20
00:01:06.416 --> 00:01:09.676
A short, or overheating, shuts its channel down.

21
00:01:09.898 --> 00:01:12.318
SKU-5 is pre-silicon.

22
00:01:12.318 --> 00:01:17.298
Every figure is a target, and ESD survival has no silicon data

23
00:01:17.298 --> 00:01:17.918
yet

24
00:01:18.918 --> 00:01:22.938
Bring us your cable, your node count and your fault cases.

25
00:01:22.938 --> 00:01:26.638
We will tell you what SKU-5 must prove.
